## Basic Row and Column

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const LayoutExampleApp());
}

class LayoutExampleApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const LayoutExampleApp({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const MaterialApp(
      debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false,
      home: LayoutHomePage(),
    );
  }
}

class LayoutHomePage extends StatelessWidget {
  const LayoutHomePage({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: const Text('Flutter Layout'),
      ),
      body: const Padding(
        padding: EdgeInsets.all(24),
        child: Column(
          crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,
          children: [
            Text(
              'Profile',
              style: TextStyle(
                fontSize: 28,
                fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
              ),
            ),
            SizedBox(height: 24),
            Row(
              children: [
                CircleAvatar(
                  radius: 32,
                  child: Icon(Icons.person),
                ),
                SizedBox(width: 16),
                Expanded(
                  child: Column(
                    crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,
                    children: [
                      Text(
                        'Flutter Developer',
                        style: TextStyle(
                          fontSize: 18,
                          fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
                        ),
                      ),
                      SizedBox(height: 4),
                      Text(
                        'Building production Flutter applications',
                      ),
                    ],
                  ),
                ),
              ],
            ),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

## Understanding the Layout

The relevant tree is:

```text
Column
├── Text
└── Row
    ├── CircleAvatar
    ├── SizedBox
    └── Expanded
        └── Column
            ├── Text
            └── Text
```

The outer `Column` arranges content vertically.

The `Row` arranges the avatar and information horizontally.

The `Expanded` takes the remaining horizontal space.

## MainAxisAlignment Example

```dart
Row(
  mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.spaceBetween,
  children: [
    const Text('Products'),
    TextButton(
      onPressed: () {},
      child: const Text('View All'),
    ),
  ],
)
```

This is a common screen-header pattern.

## CrossAxisAlignment Example

```dart
Column(
  crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,
  children: [
    const Text(
      'Title',
      style: TextStyle(fontSize: 24),
    ),
    const Text('Description'),
  ],
)
```

The children align toward the start of the horizontal cross axis.

## Expanded Example

```dart
Row(
  children: [
    Expanded(
      child: Container(
        height: 100,
        color: Colors.blue,
      ),
    ),
    const SizedBox(width: 12),
    Expanded(
      flex: 2,
      child: Container(
        height: 100,
        color: Colors.red,
      ),
    ),
  ],
)
```

The available width after the spacing is distributed in a `1 : 2` ratio.

## Flexible Example

```dart
Row(
  children: [
    Flexible(
      child: Container(
        color: Colors.blue,
        child: const Text('Flexible content'),
      ),
    ),
    const SizedBox(width: 16),
    const Icon(Icons.star),
  ],
)
```

`Flexible` allows the child to use no more than its allocated flex space without requiring the child to fill the entire allocation.

## Padding vs SizedBox

Use `Padding` when you want space around a widget:

```dart
Padding(
  padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),
  child: const Text('Content'),
)
```

Use `SizedBox` for explicit spacing:

```dart
const SizedBox(height: 16),
```

This communicates intent clearly.

## Container Example

```dart
Container(
  padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),
  decoration: BoxDecoration(
    color: Colors.blue.shade50,
    borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(16),
    border: Border.all(
      color: Colors.blue,
    ),
  ),
  child: const Text('Profile information'),
)
```

## Align Example

```dart
SizedBox(
  height: 200,
  child: Align(
    alignment: Alignment.bottomRight,
    child: FloatingActionButton(
      onPressed: () {},
      child: const Icon(Icons.add),
    ),
  ),
)
```

The `SizedBox` establishes the available area, and `Align` positions the child within it.

## Diagnosing a Row Overflow

Problem:

```dart
Row(
  children: [
    const Text('Very long product name that may not fit'),
    const Text('₹9999'),
  ],
)
```

If the text requires more width than the row can provide, an overflow can occur.

A common solution is:

```dart
Row(
  children: [
    const Expanded(
      child: Text(
        'Very long product name that may not fit',
        overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis,
      ),
    ),
    const SizedBox(width: 12),
    const Text('₹9999'),
  ],
)
```

Now the product name receives the remaining width and can truncate its text.

## Diagnosing Unbounded Height

Avoid blindly doing this:

```dart
Column(
  children: [
    Expanded(
      child: ListView(),
    ),
  ],
)
```

without first understanding the parent constraints.

Inside a bounded screen, this can be a valid pattern because the `Column` can distribute its finite available height to `Expanded`.

But placing the same structure inside another vertically scrolling parent can produce unbounded-height problems.

The correct solution depends on the entire constraint chain.

## Practical Layout Exercise

Build a product card:

```text
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Image │ Product Name       ₹999     │
│       │ Description                 │
│       │ [ Add to Cart ]             │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Use:

```text
Card
└── Padding
    └── Row
        ├── Image
        └── Expanded
            └── Column
                ├── Row
                │   ├── Product Name
                │   └── Price
                ├── Description
                └── Align
                    └── Button
```

The goal is not simply to make the screenshot look correct.

The goal is to understand why each parent gives each child its particular constraints.
